


Maren restarted the timer.




He looked at the emitter. Its small green indicator remained steady. “Did you send me away from here?”




Maren restarted the timer.




At eight seconds, the branch struck the roof.




At thirteen, a click came from the leaves near the trail.




At twenty-one, Jonah inhaled and held it.




The interval ended.




“No distinct response,” Maren said.




“You heard the click.”




“Yes.”




“After I asked.”




Maren looked at the stopwatch. “Next question.”




He kept looking at her.




Maren waited.




“Then what are we doing?”




“Recording enough silence to know when the sound changes.”




His mouth tightened. He faced the fireplace again.




For the next five questions, they followed the timing. Jonah asked whether anyone remembered the shelter, whether anyone wanted them to leave, whether the sound had been a warning, whether something had followed him home, and whether it was standing outside the light. Maren logged the branch, two vehicles, a burst of wingbeats beyond the fixed camera, and repeated grit movement when Jonah shifted his boots. Maren could not separate any response from the noise.




The generator’s vibration traveled through the concrete and into the table legs. Maren moved one microphone onto a folded cloth. The low hum weakened in that channel but remained in the others. She filmed the change and stated it aloud.




Jonah watched her adjust the cable. “Are we going to count anything that happens while it’s on?”




“We can count that it happened.”




“That isn’t what I asked.”




“We can’t pretend the equipment isn’t part of the room.”




“It was part of the room last time.”




“It was part of what we failed to record last time.”




He looked down at the emitter. “You keep using that word.”




“Failed?”




“Last time.”




Maren waited.




He touched the loose edge of his bandage but did not lift it. “You keep calling it ‘last time’ like it stopped when we left. It didn’t.”




The camera remained between them. Maren could see the tally light reflected in his eyes.




“Ask the next question,” she said.




His chair scraped backward. He stood and moved to the fireplace, staying within the camera frame.




“Are you still with me?”




The cry cut through the generator and emitter noise.




It began low, rose into a strained note, and broke once near the middle. The second part lasted longer than the first. Maren felt her shoulders draw upward before she understood that she had stopped breathing.




Jonah did not move.




The sound ended.




Only the generator and emitter remained for one beat. Then the insects filled the clearing again.




“That’s it,” Jonah said.




Maren raised one hand. “Don’t say anything yet.”




“You heard it.”




“Write your first impression.”




“That was the same sound.”




“Write what you heard before we talk.”




He stared at her, then crossed to the table and took the pencil. His left hand moved quickly over the back of the route sheet.




Maren turned the camera toward the open sides of the shelter. The fixed camera still covered the trail entrance. Nothing crossed its infrared image. She checked the recorder time and spoke without describing a source.




“Seven fifty-eight, forty-one. Distant vocalization. Two parts with a break near the middle. Direction uncertain.”




“West,” Jonah said.




“Write west.”




“It came from where it came from before.”




“You changed that direction in the car.”




“I changed where we were standing.”




Maren took a blank equipment label from the case and wrote before his wording could shape hers: long call; low to high; break; second part sustained; no words; distance unknown. She added: first impression—beyond clearing, possibly river side. She kept the label in her hand.




Jonah held up the route sheet. “I wrote scream.”




“That’s your first impression.”




“You didn’t?”




“I wrote call.”




“Why?”




“Because I don’t know what made it.”




He looked past her toward the fixed camera. “You flinched.”




Maren folded the label and put it in her pocket.




Jonah lowered the page.




Maren said nothing.




The generator coughed once.




It recovered and returned to its previous rhythm. Maren checked the fuel line and the audio levels. The cry had registered across all three recorders, strongest at the microphone near the trail. The shelter microphones carried more echo and less of the first low note.




She did not play it back.




“Again,” Jonah said toward the darkness. “Can you do that again?”




Maren looked at him. “Ask for a sound. Not the same one.”




Jonah’s face tightened.




He turned away from her.




Maren waited.




He faced the darkness.




“Can you make a sound?”




They waited.




Ten seconds passed.




A short scrape seemed to come from behind the shelter.




Jonah looked toward the fireplace. Maren swung the camera after his movement. The beam crossed rough stone, the empty hearth, leaves, bottle caps, and pale grit. Nothing moved.




A second scrape seemed to come from the open side to her right.




She turned too late to locate it. The fixed camera showed the trail and one stone pillar. The area beside the shelter remained outside its frame.




“Two scuffs,” Jonah said. “It moved.”




Maren kept the camera on the open side.




Jonah kept watching the shelter.




She kept filming.




Maren heard a third sound beneath the table, softer and longer than the first two.




Both of them looked down.




The audio cable trembled against one table leg. The generator’s vibration had worked a loop of cable off the folded cloth. It touched the concrete, lifted, and touched again.




Maren crouched and filmed it. “Cable contact.”




“That wasn’t the first one.”




“No.”




“Or the second.”




“I don’t know.”




He stepped closer to the fireplace. “Are you inside the shelter?”




Maren stood. “Jonah.”




“What?”




“Wait for the interval.”




“It just answered.”




“A cable moved.”




“After two other sounds.”




She checked the time. Her right ear had begun to ring around the emitter tone, a thinner pitch that remained when she lifted that side of the headphones away. She swallowed. The pressure in the ear shifted but did not clear.




Jonah pressed two fingers below his left ear.




“What?” she asked.




“My ears.”




“Ringing?”




“Pressure. Both sides.”




“Take three steps outside.”




He stayed where he was. “You feel it too.”




“My right ear is ringing. I’ve been wearing headphones next to a generator and an emitter.”




“You weren’t wearing them when the sound happened before.”




“Neither were you.”




He stared at her for a moment, then walked through the open side. Maren followed with the camera. The air beyond the roof was cooler against the sweat along her neck. The temperature change ended within a few steps of the shelter where the ground sloped toward the river.




Jonah opened his jaw and swallowed. “It’s less out here.”




Maren removed the headphones completely. The pressure in her ear eased, though the ringing remained. She filmed their position, the shelter, and the slope.




“Possible equipment exposure,” she said. “Temperature changed outside. The ear pressure changed too.”
